## INTRODUCTION
Alcohol abuse is one of the major problems of modern societies, contributing to the development of many debilitating diseases— including acute pancreatitis (AP) and chronic pancreatitis (CP) [1, 2]. Although the incidence of pancreatic disorders is increasing globally, thus far, there is no authorised treatment available [3]. It is generally accepted that AP is initiated by toxic cytosolic ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ signals in the pancreatic acinar cells (PACs), causing autodigestion due to premature activation of intracellular proteases and failure of mitochondrial ATP production. This results in necrosis of the enzyme-producing PACs [4, 5]. Alcohol-related AP is initiated by the powerful ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ -releasing effect of fatty acid ethyl esters (FAEEs), generated inside PACs by the non-oxidative combination of ethanol (EtOH) and fatty acids (FAs). The release of ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ from intracellular stores, followed by ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ release-activated ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ entry from the extracellular fluid, causes the toxic global and sustained increase in the cytosolic ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ concentration $([C a^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}})$ , triggering alcohol-induced AP [4, 6, 7].
Pancreatic fibrosis, mediated by pancreatic stellate cells (PSCs) [1, 8], is a particularly frequent complication in alcoholic pancreatitis, but is much less common following other aetiologies [9–11]. In health, PSCs exist mainly in their quiescent phenotype, and are known to generate $\dot{\mathsf{C}}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ signals in response to physiological levels of bradykinin [4, 12, 13]. They become activated by mechanical stress or cytokines, e.g. transforming growth factor-beta (TGF- $_{\cdot\beta)}$ [12, 14, 15]. While activated PSCs play a role in tissue healing, prolonged inflammation can promote the persistent activation of these cells [12, 16, 17]. In such a scenario,
PSCs repopulate tissue and deposit excessive amounts of extracellular matrix (ECM) components, resulting in organ dysfunction, diabetes, malnutrition and may contribute to an increased risk of pancreatic cancer [18, 19].
The role of $\dot{\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ in PACs and its relationship to the pathogenesis of AP is well established [4], but the signals that trigger pancreatic fibrosis are less clear. Activated cells are a dominant fraction of PSCs present during inflammation [14, 19], and it has been suggested that alcohol may contribute to PSC activation [20]. However, so far, it is unknown whether EtOH also affects ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ homoeostasis in PSCs. Importantly, the progression from AP to fibrosis-associated CP is likely determined by the physiological consequences of PSC activation.
Here, we provide evidence that inducers of alcoholic pancreatitis act directly on PSCs, eliciting large and sustained ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ responses in quiescent PSCs that cause cell death. However, as a result of significant alterations in ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ homoeostasis, activated PSCs develop remarkable resistance to alcohol metabolites, which prevents them from dying. We also explored the mechanisms and candidates responsible for this phenomenon and discovered that the loss of the transient receptor potential ankyrin 1 channel (TRPA1) in activated PSCs is the main contributing factor. Our results prompt us to review the current dogmas on alcoholic pancreatitis to include the TRPA1 channel and TRPA1-dependent $\dot{\mathsf{C}}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ signals in PSCs as important players in the pathogenesis of this disease.
## RESULTS EtOH and fatty acids induce pathophysiological $\pmb{C a^{2+}}$ responses in hPSCs
Since pathophysiological ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ signalling in PACs directly triggers pancreatic pathology, we sought to investigate whether nonoxidative metabolites of alcohol and FAs can also affect ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ homoeostasis in hPSCs. To address this, we monitored the realtime changes in the concentration of cytosolic $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}\mathsf{\Pi}([\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}})$ of Fluo-4-loaded hPSCs (Fig. 1A), treated with EtOH and palmitoleic acid (POA) or palmitoleic acid ethyl ester (POAEE). Our cells responded to bradykinin, as was previously reported for mouse and human PSCs (Fig. 1B) [21–23]. Our new data revealed that EtOH $(200\mathsf{m}M)$ induced only a very modest increase in $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ of hPSCs (Fig. 1C, F). In contrast to EtOH alone, simultaneous application of EtOH with POA (concentrations of EtOH/POA were: $10\mathsf{m M}/10\upmu\mathsf{M},$ $25\mathsf{m M}/25\upmu\mathsf{M}_{\ast}$ , $50\mathsf{m M}/50\upmu\mathsf{M}$ and $100\mathrm{mM}/100\upmu\mathrm{M})$ caused a large and sustained elevation of $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ in hPSCs (Fig. 1D). This increase was dose-dependent and most prominent for EtOH/POA $50\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}/50\upmu\mathsf{M}$ (Fig. 1G). EtOH/POA $100\mathrm{mM}/100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ induced such a dramatic $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ overload that it killed a substantial fraction of hPSCs during the experiment, leading to the seemingly decreased response areas (Fig. 1G). Similarly, treatment with EtOH/ POAEE $100\mathrm{mM}/100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ and $200\mathsf{m M}/200\upmu\mathsf{M}$ resulted in a global and prolonged elevation of $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ in hPSCs (Fig. 1E) and the higher concentration had a more prominent effect (Fig. 1H).
In order to characterise the above ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ signals, we carried out imaging experiments in which we disrupted intracellular ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ homoeostasis either by removing extracellular ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ or by incubation of cells with cyclopiazonic acid (CPA), a reversible inhibitor of the serco-endoplasmic reticulum ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ ATPase (SERCA) (Fig. 1I) [24]. While EtOH/POA $(10\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}/10\upmu\mathsf{M},$ , $25\mathsf{m M}/25\upmu\mathsf{M}$ and $50\mathsf{m M}/50\upmu\mathsf{M})$ induced elevation of $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathsf{i}}$ in hPSCs in the absence of extracellular ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , these responses were mostly modest and, unlike in the presence of extracellular ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ , returned to the baseline levels during the time course of the experiment (Fig. 1J). The magnitude of $\bar{C}\mathsf{a}^{2+}$ responses was very similar for all tested concentrations and did not show a clear concentration dependence (Fig. 1K). Inhibition of SERCA by CPA unmasked passive ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ leak from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) and led to emptying of the ER ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ stores. After the application of $20\upmu\upmu$
CPA, both EtOH/POA $25\mathsf{m M}/25\upmu\mathsf{M}$ and $50\mathrm{m}\mathrm{M}/50\upmu\mathrm{M}$ failed to induce further ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ signals (Fig. 1L). When the treatment was applied in reverse order, EtOH/POA $25\mathsf{m M}/25\upmu\mathsf{M}$ or $50~{\mathsf{m M}}/{\mathsf{\Lambda}}$ $50\upmu\upM$ triggered a temporal $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ increase, whereas a subsequent addition of $20\upmu\upmu$ CPA did not have any effect (Fig. 1M). This suggests that EtOH/POA releases ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ primarily from the ER store.
EtOH and POA(EE) cause activation of hPSC in vivo but not in vitro
## Activation of hPSCs has a profound effect on pathophysiological $\pmb{C a^{2+}}$ responses
Since EtOH/POA(EE) induce substantial $[\mathsf{C a}^{2+}]_{\mathrm{i}}$ responses in hPSCs in vitro, and alcoholic AP is associated with the activation of PSCs in vivo, we next examined whether the phenotype transition could affect ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ homoeostasis and contribute to the development of the disease and its complications. To achieve this, we applied an in vitro model of hPSCs activated by TGF- $\cdot\beta$ $(5\mathsf{n g}/\mathsf{m l})$ either for $48\mathsf{h}$ (ahPSC $48\mathsf{h}$ ) or 7 days (ahPSC 7 days). The phenotype of hPSCs was confirmed by immunofluorescence staining for α-SMA (Fig. 3A–C). After $48\mathsf{h}$ incubation, $\sim60\%$ of cells were positive for $\mathtt{a}$ -SMA; and after 7 days, essentially all hPSCs were activated (Fig. 3D). ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ elevation induced by EtOH/ POA $10\mathsf{m M/10\upmu M}$ was almost completely abolished in ahPSCs $48\mathsf{h}$ and 7 d compared to qhPSCs (Fig. 3E–H). EtOH/POA $25~{\mathsf{m M}}/$ $25\upmu\upmu$ induced substantially reduced $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ responses in ahPSCs (Fig. 3I–L); and a similar reduction was observed in ahPSCs treated with EtOH/POA $50\mathsf{m M}/50\upmu\mathsf{M}$ (Fig. 3M–P). Very notable attenuation of ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ responses also occurred in ahPSCs $48\mathsf{h}$ and 7 d treated with EtOH/POAEE $100\mathrm{mM}/100\upmu\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. 3Q–T) and $200\mathsf{m M}/200\upmu\mathsf{M}$ (Fig. 3U–X). For most of the above treatments, ${\mathsf{C a}}^{2+}$ signals in ahPSCs were not only reduced, but also more transient, with a tendency to return to the basal levels in the time course of the experiment.
## Expression of $\pmb{C a^{2+}}$ channels and transporters changes in ahPSCs
